---
import Base from "../../layouts/Base.astro";

const title = "Equity vs Debt — What Each Is Actually For";
const description =
  "The structural difference between owning and lending, what each asset class " +
  "does well, and why horizon rather than temperament should decide the split.";
---

<Base title={title} description={description} crumb="Investing / Equity vs debt">
  <h1>Equity versus debt</h1>
  <p class="lede">
    Equity means owning part of a business. Debt means lending money for a fixed
    return. Almost every practical difference between them follows from that one
    structural fact.
  </p>

  <h2>What follows from ownership</h2>
  <div class="defs">
    <div class="def">
      <div class="h"><code>unlimited_upside</code><span class="meta">equity</span></div>
      <p>An owner keeps whatever is left after everyone else is paid. If the
        business does well, there is no ceiling on that.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="def">
      <div class="h"><code>last_in_line</code><span class="meta">equity</span></div>
      <p>And no floor. Lenders, employees and suppliers are paid first. In
        liquidation, equity holders receive what remains, which is frequently
        nothing.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="def">
      <div class="h"><code>fixed_return_capped</code><span class="meta">debt</span></div>
      <p>A lender receives the agreed rate regardless of how well the borrower
        does. Spectacular success pays the lender exactly the same.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="def">
      <div class="h"><code>paid_first</code><span class="meta">debt</span></div>
      <p>Interest is a contractual obligation, not a discretionary payment. That
        is the whole of why debt is safer.</p>
    </div>
  </div>

  <h2>Horizon decides the split, not temperament</h2>
  <p class="lede">
    The common framing is "risk appetite". A more useful framing is
    <strong>when you need the money</strong>. Equity's variability only becomes a
    problem if you are forced to sell during a fall — and what forces you to sell
    is needing the money, not disliking volatility.
  </p>
  <div class="defs">
    <div class="def">
      <div class="h"><code>under_3_years</code><span class="meta">debt, essentially always</span></div>
      <p>Equity can be down 30% over any three-year window. Money with a near-term
        purpose should not be exposed to that, whatever your appetite.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="def">
      <div class="h"><code>3_to_7_years</code><span class="meta">mixed</span></div>
      <p>Long enough to recover from an ordinary fall, not long enough to be
        confident of it.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="def">
      <div class="h"><code>over_10_years</code><span class="meta">equity-weighted</span></div>
      <p>Long enough that the main risk shifts from volatility to inflation — and
        against inflation, debt is the riskier choice. See
        <a href="/learn/inflation/">inflation</a>.</p>
    </div>
  </div>

  <h2>The risk that changes places</h2>
  <p class="lede">
    Over one year, equity is far riskier. Over thirty, the position reverses: a
    portfolio of fixed deposits earning a post-tax 5% against 6% inflation loses
    purchasing power with complete reliability. Equity <em>might</em>; debt at
    those rates <em>will</em>. <strong>Which asset is "safe" depends entirely on
    the horizon.</strong>
  </p>

  <h2>Practical notes for India</h2>
  <div class="defs">
    <div class="def">
      <div class="h"><code>taxation_differs</code><span class="meta">verify current rules</span></div>
      <p>Equity and debt instruments are taxed differently, and the rules have
        changed more than once in recent years. Check the current position rather
        than relying on anything remembered — it materially affects any post-tax
        comparison.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="def">
      <div class="h"><code>debt_is_not_risk_free</code><span class="meta">two real risks</span></div>
      <p>Credit risk: the borrower may not repay. Interest-rate risk: existing
        bonds fall in value when rates rise. Debt mutual funds carry both, which
        surprises people who bought them as the safe option.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="def">
      <div class="h"><code>epf_and_ppf_are_debt</code><span class="meta">often forgotten</span></div>
      <p>Most salaried Indians already hold a substantial debt allocation through
        provident fund contributions. Count it before concluding you need more.</p>
    </div>
  </div>

  <div class="ad">Advertisement</div>

  <p class="lede">
    Next: <a href="/learn/asset-allocation/">asset allocation</a> ·
    <a href="/learn/inflation/">inflation</a> ·
    <a href="/mutual-funds/">mutual funds</a>
  </p>
</Base>
